Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["FindInfraParallel", "ObjectName",
 CellID->1224892054,ExpressionUUID->"8393d601-4014-44ba-afc9-60862df940ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"6a3af742-8207-4c95-ac24-0791cca6d8d4"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["FindInfraParallel"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/FindInfraParallel", 
     "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["line", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["p", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9d5ff5eb-4d37-4252-84b1-70085fe64b26"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "gives one parallel to ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " in ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 " \[LongDash] a geodesic through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " that stays at the distance ",
 StyleBox["d(p, line)", "TI"],
 " from ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " and cannot be prolonged at that distance \[LongDash] as an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraLine"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraLine", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8767594c-db6d-43fe-ae78-efdfcaae5238"],
 " wrapper.",
 "\n",
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"41b87bcd-29c4-4e07-a21f-5c196f136aa5"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["FindInfraParallel"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/FindInfraParallel", 
     "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["line", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "218b7e01-e2d4-4d61-95ab-fd64a2526f9c"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "gives exactly ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 " parallels or ",
 Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "c490fefa-e71a-440d-bbd0-513a337e5ad5"],
 "; ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"UpTo", "[", "n", "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "f062047e-0082-4fc4-ba25-e9352825a8b3"],
 " gives up to ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 "; ",
 Cell[BoxData["All"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3be8fb47-8ea6-45c4-a30b-773a4464fccb"],
 " gives the whole class."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"75c3a721-2318-4943-9ac4-148b27143efa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Write ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 " for the level set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     StyleBox["v", "TI"], ":", 
     StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       StyleBox["v", "TI"], ",", 
       StyleBox["l", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["i", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["e", "TI"]}], ")"}], "\[LongEqual]", 
     StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", 
       StyleBox["l", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["i", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["e", "TI"]}], ")"}]}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "427cd70c-02a8-4aff-9fd7-e751a6e48584"],
 ". A parallel to ",
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " through ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " is a geodesic ",
 StyleBox["s \[Ellipsis] p \[Ellipsis] e", "TI"],
 " of ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 " \[LongDash] so ",
 StyleBox["d(s, e) = d(s, p) + d(p, e)", "TI"],
 " \[LongDash] with every vertex in ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 ", such that no neighbour of ",
 StyleBox["s", "TI"],
 " or ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 " in ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 " prolongs it. This reads Euclid's fifth postulate intrinsically: constant \
distance is what a metric can say about parallelism without a notion of \
direction. Inextensibility is measured inside ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 ", not in ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 ", so a parallel need not be a line of ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"512c6cc0-e9f0-4a91-9ef6-eefbb9f38fbe"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["line", "TI"],
 " is a vertex sequence or an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraLine"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraLine", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2935ef04-9d9e-4be9-ba4b-0bdd911f8bb6"],
 " wrapper, which spreads over its realisations. Each parallel appears once, \
oriented so that ",
 StyleBox["s", "TI"],
 " precedes ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 " in canonical order."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"304ff293-763e-406c-b869-2a9ca122483d"],

Cell["\<\
On the square grid the classical picture survives: through a vertex of a \
parallel row there is exactly one parallel to a row, and it is the row. Off a \
line that bends, the level set bends with it, and a geodesic inside it soon \
has to leave \[LongDash] the parallels are then few and short. Below, through \
a vertex at distance 2 from a line through the centre, the square tiling has \
one parallel of 10 edges beside a line of 24, the irregular mesh two of 4 and \
3 edges, the hexagonal tiling a single edge. A vertex with no neighbour in \
its level set has no parallel at all: the class is empty, not the one-vertex \
walk.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"972c7926-5142-4ef6-bca4-3642afc217d9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraParallelQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraParallelQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "30a8f33f-6973-4910-b98c-e7d9e60348cd"],
 " asks a different question, and the two do not match. The predicate tests \
that two vertex sets are disjoint and at constant distance, and does not \
require either to be a geodesic. So it accepts pairs this function never \
returns \[LongDash] concentric shells, for instance, are parallel by that \
test \[LongDash] while every parallel returned here passes it."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f4d71278-d26c-4391-a3d8-e80bfa791173"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The class is carried by a ",
 StyleBox["pool",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": one geodesic DAG per admissible pair of ends (",
 StyleBox["s", "TI"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 "), the ",
 StyleBox["s \[RightArrow] p", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["p \[RightArrow] e", "TI"],
 " intervals cut down to ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 " and glued at ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData["All"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1f153f87-802e-4630-90cb-ac4658a9debf"],
 " reads every atom into explicit vertex sequences, and a bounded count \
streams parallels off the atoms \[LongDash] the count-less call is one \
parallel, deterministic. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"[", "\"\<Length\>\"", "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1c5268ab-e849-4504-bfe0-f7926beee72c"],
 " gives one edge count per parallel."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"68addeda-4016-46b8-867c-6f9b220386df"],

